impulse. The units of impulse are kilograms meters per second which is the same as momentum

00:45:35.970 --> 00:45:41.930
which makes sense because that&#x27;s the quantities in momentum or Newton&#x27;s seconds which makes sense

00:45:41.930 --> 00:45:49.290
because the units of F times t is n s. It&#x27;s also a vector quantity. So the key to remembering about

00:45:49.290 --> 00:45:55.990
impulse is that there&#x27;s two expressions we can use to find the impulse of a collision or a change

00:45:55.990 --> 00:46:02.390
of momentum. Why does this matter? Well impulse you see it everywhere in real life once you understand

00:46:02.390 --> 00:46:11.270
it you see it everywhere. Let&#x27;s think about a car crashing so we&#x27;ve got this car heads towards the

00:46:11.270 --> 00:46:20.970
wall it&#x27;s got mass m and velocity v so it&#x27;s got an initial momentum hits the wall okay sadly and

00:46:20.970 --> 00:46:28.490
its final momentum is equal to zero. There&#x27;s a change in momentum there. That change in momentum

00:46:28.490 --> 00:46:34.530
is constant because it starts off with a constant mass and a constant velocity and it ends up with

00:46:34.530 --> 00:46:40.230
quantities that aren&#x27;t changed either. The change in momentum this value here is constant.

00:46:41.670 --> 00:46:48.530
What that means is if you can increase the time taken for any collision then you must be able to

00:46:48.530 --> 00:46:55.030
