collision. Now that is not going to be you. Let&#x27;s look at inelastic and elastic collisions.

00:50:28.570 --> 00:50:34.870
We know from previously that momentum is conserved in all collisions assuming no

00:50:34.870 --> 00:50:41.190
external forces apply. We can make lots of calculations with that simple knowledge. However

00:50:41.190 --> 00:50:48.570
if you consider a collision kinetic energy is not conserved because when a collision happens it&#x27;s

00:50:48.570 --> 00:50:55.290
going to lose energy. It&#x27;s going to lose sound energy and heat energy and if that happens

00:50:55.290 --> 00:51:02.810
then this collision is inelastic. If it loses kinetic energy during a collision then it&#x27;s

00:51:02.810 --> 00:51:11.790
inelastic. I&#x27;ve got news for you most collisions and most explosions are inelastic. An elastic collision

00:51:13.110 --> 00:51:19.350
is one where kinetic energy is conserved so if you take the kinetic energy of the ball here

00:51:21.210 --> 00:51:28.230
before the collision down here and you take the kinetic energy of the ball here after the collision

00:51:28.950 --> 00:51:34.730
these two kinetic energies would be exactly the same. Can you imagine this happening in real life?

00:51:35.250 --> 00:51:40.510
Not particularly, no. Energy is lost in most collisions and explosions. So examples where

00:51:40.510 --> 00:51:47.210
