earlier but we didn&#x27;t have a formula to tell you exactly what magnitude of that acceleration

01:01:26.820 --> 01:01:34.400
was. Now we have a formula. The centripetal acceleration is a equals v squared over r

01:01:35.120 --> 01:01:41.340
and it has meters per second squared units just like in linear motion. But we also know

01:01:41.340 --> 01:01:50.280
that v equals omega r and if we substitute that into here we end up with a range of different

01:01:50.280 --> 01:01:56.980
expressions for centripetal acceleration. Similarly Newton&#x27;s second law states that

01:01:56.980 --> 01:02:02.900
the resultant force equals mass times acceleration in the direction of the force. So if we have

01:02:02.900 --> 01:02:08.720
acceleration towards the center we have force towards the center and the force is equal to

01:02:11.000 --> 01:02:18.740
a. That means we can add f equals m a to our acceleration equations and we end up with these

01:02:18.740 --> 01:02:25.680
equations for centripetal force. The force is always measured in Newton&#x27;s. If an object

01:02:25.680 --> 01:02:32.320
is in circular motion there&#x27;s a force towards the center of the circular motion then that

01:02:32.320 --> 01:02:37.960
force has to have a name. We talked about forces earlier. We talked about all sorts of different

01:02:37.960 --> 01:02:43.420
forces. This force has to have a name. It can&#x27;t just be a centripetal force. It has to

01:02:43.420 --> 01:02:50.340
have a force that&#x27;s causing this centripetal force and one of the skills of IB physics is identifying

01:02:50.340 --> 01:02:55.860
the type of force. Now if we&#x27;re looking at a satellite going round earth the type of

01:02:55.860 --> 01:03:01.720
force that&#x27;s causing that centripetal motion is a gravitational force. If we&#x27;re looking at

01:03:01.720 --> 01:03:07.600
a ball being swung around somebody&#x27;s head on a string then this force here is tension.

